[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Spring Data JDBC: A Lightweight Alternative to JPA"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spring Data JDBC is a module within the Spring Data umbrella project that provides a simplified approach to relational database access compared to the more comprehensive JPA (Java Persistence API). It focuses on simplicity and direct mapping between database tables and Java objects, offering a less opinionated and more predictable way to interact with relational databases in Spring Boot applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Why Choose Spring Data JDBC?"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Simplicity: Easier to understand and use, especially for simpler data models."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Performance: Can offer better performance in some scenarios due to its direct mapping approach and reduced overhead compared to JPA's more complex object-relational mapping."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Control: Gives you more control over the generated SQL queries."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Less Magic: More explicit and predictable behavior, making debugging easier."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting Up Spring Data JDBC in a Spring Boot Project"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, you need to add the Spring Data JDBC dependency to your `pom.xml` (for Maven) or `build.gradle` (for Gradle)."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"<!-- Maven -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jdbc</artifactId>
</dependency>

<!-- Gradle -->
dependencies {
    implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-data-jdbc'
}"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, configure your database connection in `application.properties` or `application.yml`. Here's an example using H2, an in-memory database, for simplicity:"},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"# application.properties
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb
spring.datasource.driverClassName=org.h2.Driver
spring.datasource.username=sa
spring.datasource.password=
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining Entities"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Entities in Spring Data JDBC are simple POJOs (Plain Old Java Objects) annotated with `@Id` to mark the primary key.  Relationships between entities are handled more explicitly than in JPA."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import org.springframework.data.annotation.Id;

public class Product {

    @Id
    private Long id;
    private String name;
    private double price;

    public Product() {}

    public Product(String name, double price) {
        this.name = name;
        this.price = price;
    }

    public Long getId() {
        return id;
    }

    public void setId(Long id) {
        this.id = id;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public double getPrice() {
        return price;
    }

    public void setPrice(double price) {
        this.price = price;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Product{" +
                "id=" + id +
                ", name='" + name + '\'' +
                ", price=" + price +
                '}';
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating Repositories"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Repositories are interfaces that extend `CrudRepository` or `PagingAndSortingRepository`. Spring Data JDBC automatically generates the implementation for common database operations."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"import org.springframework.data.repository.CrudRepository;

public interface ProductRepository extends CrudRepository<Product, Long> {
    // You can add custom query methods here if needed
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Using the Repository"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can inject the repository into your Spring components and use it to perform database operations."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.CommandLineRunner;
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;

@SpringBootApplication
public class DemoApplication {

    @Autowired
    private ProductRepository productRepository;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(DemoApplication.class, args);
    }

    @Bean
    public CommandLineRunner demo() {
        return (args) -> {
            // Save a few products
            productRepository.save(new Product("Laptop", 1200.00));
            productRepository.save(new Product("Mouse", 25.00));

            // Fetch all products
            System.out.println("Products found with findAll():");
            System.out.println("------------------------------");
            productRepository.findAll().forEach(System.out::println);

            // Fetch an individual product by ID
            Product product = productRepository.findById(1L).orElse(null);
            System.out.println("\nProduct found with findById(1L):");
            System.out.println("--------------------------------");
            System.out.println(product);
        };
    }
}"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Relationships"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spring Data JDBC handles relationships differently than JPA.  It relies on explicit mapping and aggregate roots.  Let's consider a one-to-many relationship between `Customer` and `Order`."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"import org.springframework.data.annotation.Id;
import org.springframework.data.relational.core.mapping.MappedCollection;

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class Customer {

    @Id
    private Long id;
    private String firstName;
    private String lastName;

    @MappedCollection(idColumn = "customer_id")
    private Set<Order> orders = new HashSet<>();

    public Customer() {}

    public Customer(String firstName, String lastName) {
        this.firstName = firstName;
        this.lastName = lastName;
    }

    // Getters and setters

    public Long getId() {
        return id;
    }

    public void setId(Long id) {
        this.id = id;
    }

    public String getFirstName() {
        return firstName;
    }

    public void setFirstName(String firstName) {
        this.firstName = firstName;
    }

    public String getLastName() {
        return lastName;
    }

    public void setLastName(String lastName) {
        this.lastName = lastName;
    }

    public Set<Order> getOrders() {
        return orders;
    }

    public void setOrders(Set<Order> orders) {
        this.orders = orders;
    }

    public void addOrder(Order order) {
        this.orders.add(order);
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Customer{" +
                "id=" + id +
                ", firstName='" + firstName + '\'' +
                ", lastName='" + lastName + '\'' +
                ", orders=" + orders +
                '}';
    }
}"},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"import org.springframework.data.annotation.Id;

public class Order {

    @Id
    private Long id;
    private String orderNumber;

    // No direct reference to Customer here!

    public Order() {}

    public Order(String orderNumber) {
        this.orderNumber = orderNumber;
    }

    // Getters and setters

    public Long getId() {
        return id;
    }

    public void setId(Long id) {
        this.id = id;
    }

    public String getOrderNumber() {
        return orderNumber;
    }

    public void setOrderNumber(String orderNumber) {
        this.orderNumber = orderNumber;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Order{" +
                "id=" + id +
                ", orderNumber='" + orderNumber + '\'' +
                '}';
    }
}"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Key points about the relationship mapping:"},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`@MappedCollection(idColumn = "customer_id")` on the `orders` field in the `Customer` class indicates that the `Order` entities are part of the `Customer` aggregate. The `idColumn` specifies the foreign key column in the `Order` table that references the `Customer` table."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The `Order` class *does not* have a direct field referencing the `Customer`.  The relationship is managed through the aggregate root (`Customer`) and the `customer_id` column in the `Order` table."},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you save a `Customer` object with associated `Order` objects, Spring Data JDBC will automatically handle saving the `Order` objects and setting the `customer_id` in the `Order` table."},{"order":30,"type":"code","text":"import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.CommandLineRunner;
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;

@SpringBootApplication
public class DemoApplication {

    @Autowired
    private CustomerRepository customerRepository;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(DemoApplication.class, args);
    }

    @Bean
    public CommandLineRunner demo(CustomerRepository customerRepository) {
        return (args) -> {
            Customer customer = new Customer("John", "Doe");
            customer.addOrder(new Order("ORD-123"));
            customer.addOrder(new Order("ORD-456"));

            customerRepository.save(customer);

            Customer retrievedCustomer = customerRepository.findById(customer.getId()).orElse(null);
            System.out.println("Retrieved Customer: " + retrievedCustomer);
        };
    }
}

interface CustomerRepository extends CrudRepository<Customer, Long> {}
"},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"Custom Queries"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"While Spring Data JDBC provides basic CRUD operations, you may need to execute custom queries. You can achieve this using `@Query` annotation."},{"order":33,"type":"code","text":"import org.springframework.data.jdbc.repository.query.Query;
import org.springframework.data.repository.CrudRepository;
import org.springframework.data.repository.query.Param;

import java.util.List;

public interface ProductRepository extends CrudRepository<Product, Long> {

    @Query("SELECT * FROM product WHERE price > :price")
    List<Product> findProductsWithPriceGreaterThan(@Param("price") double price);
}"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `findProductsWithPriceGreaterThan` method is annotated with `@Query`, which specifies the SQL query to execute. The `@Param` annotation binds the method parameter `price` to the `:price` placeholder in the query."},{"order":35,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":36,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spring Data JDBC offers a lightweight and straightforward alternative to JPA for relational database access in Spring Boot applications. Its simplicity, performance characteristics, and explicit mapping approach make it a compelling choice for projects where you need more control over your database interactions and prefer a less opinionated framework."}]
